15.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {C}

The experiment involves light-dependent covalent protein labeling (detected via downstream CuAAC with Cy5-azide) that is far stronger with the 4-hydroxyphenyl probe than with the 4-(hydroxymethyl)phenyl analog. The common photosensitizer (A) and the final detection reagent (E) are unchanged between the two probes, eliminating them as the source of the observed difference. A phenoxyl radical (B) can only be formed from the phenolic probe; the benzylic alcohol cannot generate it. A carbene (D) is not produced under these conditions.

The bicyclo[4.2.0]octadiene scaffold bearing the 7-carboxylate/8-carboxamide substitution is predisposed to cyclobutane fragmentation, directly furnishing the electrophilic Michael acceptor methyl (E)-4-oxo-4-(prop-2-yn-1-ylamino)but-2-enoate (C). This species can alkylate nucleophilic residues on proteins independently of the sensitizer-mediated radical pathway. Consequently a weak but still detectable light-dependent increase in Cy5 signal persists with the second probe, albeit much diminished relative to the phenolic case. Thus C is the species responsible for the residual fluorescent difference.}

