11.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {The core observation is strong light-dependent protein labeling (read out by Cy5 click) with the 4-hydroxyphenyl probe that collapses sharply when the phenol is replaced by a 4-(hydroxymethyl)phenyl group. This implicates a phenol-specific pathway (phenoxyl-radical formation via the thioxanthenone sensitizer) as the dominant mechanism. The residual, weaker light-vs-dark difference with the second probe must therefore arise from a secondary pathway that does not require the phenolic OH.  

Colleagues are split: majority favor A (direct action of the shared thioxanthenone photosensitizer via background H-abstraction/energy transfer), a minority favor B (claiming residual phenoxyl-like radicals from the benzylic alcohol) or C (retro-[2+2] fragmentation to the Michael acceptor). B is mechanistically implausible—phenoxyl radicals require an Ar–OH; benzylic alcohols yield benzylic radicals instead. C is not shown to be light- or sensitizer-dependent under these conditions. E is added after irradiation and is identical in all lanes.  

Thus the only species common to both experiments whose photo-excitation can still produce a (much weaker) light-dependent signal is the thioxanthenone (A). This matches the wavelength, the strict light dependence, and the structural change between probes. No unresolved mechanistic details alter the choice.}

