11.text:
PRIVATE REFLECTION: {Core consensus after round 1: overwhelming majority (all but a few holdouts) now converge on A as the source of the residual light-dependent Cy5 signal with the second probe. Key preserved evidence: phenol probe drives dominant, efficient phenoxyl-radical pathway (thioxanthenone-sensitized H-abstraction/oxidation at 417 nm) yielding large light-vs-dark labeling difference; benzyl-alcohol swap removes that donor, collapsing the difference but leaving a weaker yet observable light-dependent increment.  

Strong arguments preserved/elaborated: only A is (i) unchanged across probes, (ii) absorbs 417 nm, and (iii) can still initiate background H-abstraction/energy/electron transfer or nonspecific oxidation once the phenol-specific channel is eliminated. B is eliminated because phenoxyl radicals require an Ar–OH absent in the second probe. C (Michael acceptor from retro-[2+2]) is inconsistent with strict light dependence and phenol-modulated drop—fragmentation would be thermal/ground-state, appear equally in dark controls, and be insensitive to the para-substituent. D lacks any carbene precursor; E is added post-irradiation and identical in every lane.  

No unresolved mechanistic details alter the choice; residual pathway is simply lower-efficiency sensitizer-only reactivity. My prior reflection is reinforced; the round shows convergence with explicit rebuttals of C and B.}

